  "subtitle": "A complete computer terminal in a briefcase — with a telephone handset as its network port",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Silent 700 was a self-contained portable terminal: keyboard, thermal printer, and acoustic coupler in a single 13-pound briefcase. To go online, you opened the lid, dialed a number on a standard telephone, and when you heard the carrier tone, you pressed the handset into the molded rubber cups built into the terminal's case. The terminal printed received data in near-silence on heat-sensitive paper at 30 characters per second — no CRT, no separate modem, no external cables except a power cord. Field engineers, journalists, and early CompuServe subscribers used it as their window into remote mainframes and time-sharing systems. It represented a moment when 'going online' was a physical ritual involving acoustic handshakes and thermal paper, before networking became invisible.",
